<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<odoo>
    <!-- 20: the rules live in ir.access.csv, with the ACL rows; this file keeps the groups. --><data noupdate="0">

        <!--
            Mailbox manager.

            Splits "may configure mailboxes and accounts" off from
            base.group_system, so granting it no longer means granting full
            system administration. Implied by base.group_system, so existing
            administrators keep every right they had before this module version.
        -->
        <!--
            What it grants: configuring mailboxes, email accounts and the
            internal domain list. (It also gated the incoming mail triage
            queue, which 19.0.7.0.0 removed.)

            Only `name` is set. Odoo 19 reworked res.groups and `category_id`
            no longer exists on it — setting it does not warn, it fails the
            module install outright.
        -->
        <record id="group_mail_mailbox_manager" model="res.groups">
            <field name="name">Mailbox Manager</field>
        </record>

        <record id="base.group_system" model="res.groups">
            <field name="implied_ids" eval="[(4, ref('pan_mail_pro.group_mail_mailbox_manager'))]"/>
        </record>

        <!--
            A personal mailbox is a person's own address, and it carries the
            owner's identity: mail sent from it is signed by the owner's
            delegated token. Listing every colleague's personal mailbox to every
            internal user is both an information leak and the discovery half of
            the send-as problem that _is_sendable_by() closes.

            Shared and notification mailboxes stay visible to everyone: they are
            company addresses and the composer needs to offer them.
        -->
        <!--
            An account is a set of credentials. Even with the token fields
            themselves restricted to base.group_system, the row discloses who
            authorized what and when. A user needs to see their own; a service
            account (user_id unset) belongs to the company and is what makes a
            Gmail shared mailbox work, so it stays readable.
        -->
        <!--
            An access row names a sign-in and what it may do on a mailbox.
            Same shape as the account rule: a user reads the rows of their
            own sign-in and of the service accounts; a manager reads all.
        -->
        <!--
            The three tables that record what happened to mail are mailbox
            managers only, with no base.group_user access row at all.
            `pan.mail.routing.log` keeps the subject, the sender and the
            destination of every incoming mail; `pan.mail.thread.link` and
            `pan.mail.message.ref` map correspondence onto records. None of
            them inherit the ACL of the document they point at, so read access
            there hands any internal user the subject lines and correspondents
            of HR, legal and sales records they cannot open. The menu was
            restricted to base.group_system, which is not an ACL: one
            search_read over RPC returned the table. Every caller inside the
            module already goes through sudo(), so the rows are gone rather
            than narrowed.
        -->

        <!--
            A draft is a half-written answer, and it belongs to one person
            until they send it. No manager exception and no company-wide read:
            the mailbox managers who may read every mail in a shared mailbox
            still may not read what a colleague has not sent. One rule, all
            four operations, every internal user - an administrator's own
            drafts are the only ones they see here.
        -->
        </data>
</odoo>
